## Proof

**Claim 1.** If $f \in L^1(\mathbb{R})$ and $\hat f \in L^1(\mathbb{R})$, then
$$f(t) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{2\pi i \omega t}\hat f(\omega)\,d\omega \quad \text{for a.e. } t\in\mathbb{R}.$$

**Claim 2.** By redefining $f$ on a set of measure zero, the formula can be made to hold for *every* $t\in\mathbb{R}$.

---

### Setup and notation

With the convention $\hat f(\omega)=\int_{\mathbb R} f(t)\,e^{-2\pi i\omega t}\,dt$, define
$$g(t):=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{2\pi i\omega t}\hat f(\omega)\,d\omega.$$
Since $\hat f\in L^1$, the integral defining $g$ converges absolutely for every $t$, and by dominated convergence $g$ is continuous. Moreover, $|g(t)|\le\|\hat f\|_1$, so $g$ is bounded. We will show $g=f$ a.e.

---

### Key tool: approximate identity / summability kernel

For $\varepsilon>0$ let $K_\varepsilon(t)=\frac{1}{\varepsilon}K(t/\varepsilon)$ where $K(t)=e^{-\pi t^2}$ (Gaussian). Then $\widehat K(\omega)=e^{-\pi\omega^2}$ and $\widehat{K_\varepsilon}(\omega)=e^{-\pi\varepsilon^2\omega^2}$. The family $\{K_\varepsilon\}_{\varepsilon>0}$ is an approximate identity: $K_\varepsilon*f\to f$ in $L^1$ as $\varepsilon\to 0$.

A direct computation (Fubini, justified since $f\in L^1$ and $\hat f\in L^1$) gives
$$(K_\varepsilon*f)(t)=\int_{\mathbb R} e^{2\pi i\omega t}\,\widehat{K_\varepsilon}(\omega)\,\hat f(\omega)\,d\omega=\int_{\mathbb R} e^{2\pi i\omega t}\,e^{-\pi\varepsilon^2\omega^2}\,\hat f(\omega)\,d\omega.$$

---

### Passing to the limit

Since $\hat f\in L^1$ and $|e^{2\pi i\omega t}e^{-\pi\varepsilon^2\omega^2}\hat f(\omega)|\le|\hat f(\omega)|$ with $e^{-\pi\varepsilon^2\omega^2}\to 1$ pointwise, dominated convergence yields, for *every* $t$,
$$\lim_{\varepsilon\to 0}(K_\varepsilon*f)(t)=\int_{\mathbb R}e^{2\pi i\omega t}\hat f(\omega)\,d\omega=g(t).$$

On the other hand, $K_\varepsilon*f\to f$ in $L^1$. Convergence in $L^1$ implies convergence in measure, hence there is a subsequence $\varepsilon_n\to 0$ along which $(K_{\varepsilon_n}*f)(t)\to f(t)$ for a.e. $t$. Comparing the two limits,

$$f(t)=g(t)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{2\pi i\omega t}\hat f(\omega)\,d\omega \quad\text{for a.e. }t.$$

This establishes **Claim 1**.

---

### Extension to all $t$ (Claim 2)

The function $g(t)=\int e^{2\pi i\omega t}\hat f(\omega)\,d\omega$ is defined for every $t$, is continuous (dominated convergence, since $\hat f\in L^1$), and equals $f$ a.e. Define
$$\tilde f(t):=g(t).$$
Then $\tilde f=f$ a.e., i.e. $\tilde f$ is obtained from $f$ by redefining it on the null set $\{t:f(t)\ne g(t)\}$, and for *every* $t\in\mathbb R$,
$$\tilde f(t)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{2\pi i\omega t}\hat f(\omega)\,d\omega.$$

Thus the inversion formula holds pointwise everywhere after redefining $f$ on a set of measure zero. $\blacksquare$

---

**Remark.** The redefined function $\tilde f=g$ is not merely pointwise equal to the integral; it is continuous and bounded (in fact uniformly continuous, since $\hat f\in L^1$). So the "correct" representative of $f$ under the hypotheses $f,\hat f\in L^1$ is a continuous function vanishing at infinity — a strengthening of the classical result that follows from the Riemann–Lebesgue lemma applied to $\hat f$.

